WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.752 position:50% size:92% align:center
For output C at row zero and column zero, a

00:00:03.752 --> 00:00:05.731 position:50% size:92% align:center
thread combines row zero of

00:00:05.731 --> 00:00:07.491 position:50% size:92% align:center
A with column zero of B.

00:00:07.711 --> 00:00:10.284 position:50% size:92% align:center
Neighbouring outputs reuse parts of those

00:00:10.284 --> 00:00:12.481 position:50% size:92% align:center
inputs. The recording follows where

00:00:12.481 --> 00:00:14.239 position:50% size:92% align:center
that reuse happens on an SM.

00:00:16.259 --> 00:00:18.900 position:50% size:92% align:center
Four threads form a two-by-two output block.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.660 position:50% size:92% align:center
They cooperatively load a two-by-two tile from

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.301 position:50% size:92% align:center
each input into their block’s shared memory.

00:00:24.521 --> 00:00:27.066 position:50% size:92% align:center
The first block barrier completes those shared

00:00:27.066 --> 00:00:29.001 position:50% size:92% align:center
writes before the tile is consumed.

00:00:31.021 --> 00:00:34.333 position:50% size:92% align:center
Threads computing the same output row can reuse

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:37.503 position:50% size:92% align:center
an A value. Threads computing the same output

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:39.405 position:50% size:92% align:center
column can reuse a B value.

00:00:39.625 --> 00:00:42.374 position:50% size:92% align:center
Each thread keeps its own partial output sum.

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:44.389 position:50% size:92% align:center
Sharing the inputs does not merge

00:00:44.389 --> 00:00:45.855 position:50% size:92% align:center
the output accumulators.

00:00:47.875 --> 00:00:50.366 position:50% size:92% align:center
The second block barrier protects the tile from

00:00:50.366 --> 00:00:52.009 position:50% size:92% align:center
being overwritten while another

00:00:52.009 --> 00:00:53.123 position:50% size:92% align:center
thread still uses it.

00:00:53.343 --> 00:00:56.069 position:50% size:92% align:center
The block loads the next K positions and

00:00:56.069 --> 00:00:59.205 position:50% size:92% align:center
continues its private sums. The shown C at row

00:00:59.205 --> 00:01:02.409 position:50% size:92% align:center
zero, column zero finishes at three point zero.

00:01:04.429 --> 00:01:07.362 position:50% size:92% align:center
Suppose two blocks are resident on the same SM.

00:01:07.362 --> 00:01:10.107 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause and predict whether the second block’s

00:01:10.107 --> 00:01:11.604 position:50% size:92% align:center
shared array is the same

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:13.539 position:50% size:92% align:center
workspace as the first block’s.

00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:15.980 position:50% size:92% align:center
Does sharing an SM make block-scoped

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.769 position:50% size:92% align:center
arrays shared between blocks?

00:01:23.589 --> 00:01:26.533 position:50% size:92% align:center
Each block has its own shared workspace, even

00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:29.608 position:50% size:92% align:center
when both use the same SM. One block cannot use

00:01:29.608 --> 00:01:32.421 position:50% size:92% align:center
the other block’s shared array as its tile.

00:01:32.641 --> 00:01:35.247 position:50% size:92% align:center
Reuse in this kernel comes from cooperating

00:01:35.247 --> 00:01:37.732 position:50% size:92% align:center
threads within a block, while each thread

00:01:37.732 --> 00:01:39.489 position:50% size:92% align:center
retains a private output sum.
